next questions about interviews and  firstly what are they like  interviews are much feared and often  misunderstood things the purpose of an  interview is to recreate the conditions  of a tutorial or a supervision in  Cambridge to see how a candidate  performs and they formed such an  important part of the selection process  because much of the teaching done at  universities is done under the same  conditions and tutors want to choose  students who will get the most out of  that system as such interviews take the  form of a ten to thirty minute  discussion with academic about your  subject and some tutorials are done by  one academic some MOTU and others still  by panels of academics and other  officials both the universities realize  that candidates are scared of interviews  and so you are made to feel as  comfortable as you can be under the  circumstances it's also worth stating  that the tutors are not there to take  you apart to be aggressive they want to  see you do well as one of my tutor said  to me once the purpose of an interview  isn't to find out what you don't know  that would be way too easy  what it is is to find out what you do  know to find out how you think they are  trying to coax out of you how you  approach and solve problems and they  want to see you do well  they are looking for knowledge of your  subject but they're also looking for  problem solving an independent thought  form or official information about  interviews you can check out this link  for Oxford and this link for Cambridge  secondly do I need to submit work for my  interviews some subjects will require  you to do an aptitude test typically in  October or November before the interview  season in December while other subjects  will require you to submit written work  for your interviews needless to say this  vary substantially from subject to  subject and so check out the department  page for your subject for more  information about this note that some  subjects weren't required to do either  and so you won't have to do an aptitude  test or submit work thirdly how many  interviews will I have to do now this  varies a lot from subject to subject  them from person to person personally I  did four interviews at Oxford I did  three at the college that I apply to and  one at a college that was allocated to  me when I arrived and these all took  place over three days now this is a  relatively common number of interviews  and it's also very common at least for  Oxford to be allocated an additional  college when you arrive this is done to  make sure that  leave a number of applicants interviewed  across the whole University now  some interviewees were asked to stay  back for additional interviews after  their original number we're done and I  wasn't but this is again relatively  common and not something to worry about  it can be done for any number of reasons  and doesn't necessarily say anything  about your application so don't sweat it  if this happens to you a good ballpark  estimate is do you do anything between  two and six interviews though most  people will be at the lower end of that  range for more information about a  particular subjects interview at a  particular College contact that College  directly fourthly how can I best prepare  for my interviews the best way to think  about an interview is a chance to have a  short conversation with leading  academics in a field that you are  passionately interested in and so the  best way to prepare for that  conversation is to make sure you're up  to date on any topics that might come up  and that you're able to clearly  articulate your opinions and ideas if  you get the chance to have a practice  interview then take it it may not be the  most realistic recreation of the  conditions of an interview but any  practice at verbally responding to  questions in your field is great  practice in fact getting into the habit  of talking out loud your thought process  while answering questions is a fantastic  habit to get into  remember that tutors aren't interested  necessarily in you getting to the right  answer if one even exists they're  interesting at how you arrived at your  answer so practice talking through your  thought process and allow them to know  how you think also as far as it's  possible try to relax before going into  your interview  nobody does well when they're stressed  and scared so before you go and try and  get yourself into a calm and happy space  though this is easier said than done  I realize lastly if you'd like to see  how a typical interview goes you can  find several good examples on YouTube  even if the interview isn't in your  subject it gives you a general idea of  how the interview will proceed for a  good example you can check out this  computer science interview from  Cambridge by Jake right
 

